   "source": "# Predicting Search Click-Through Rate: A Data-Driven Approach for Content Optimization\n\n**Author:** Noor Ul Ain  \n**Date:** August 2026  \n**Course:** FlyRank ML Internship 2026  \n**Repository:** https://github.com/noor-meer/flyrank-ml-internship"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 1. Question\n\n*The research question and the decision it supports.*\n\n### Research Question\n\n> What factors most strongly predict whether a user will click on a search result, and how can content teams use this information to prioritize page optimization?\n\n### Decision It Supports\n\n**Decision:** Which pages should a content team prioritize for improvement?\n\n**Who Acts:** Content strategists, SEO specialists, and website editors\n\n**Action:** Review, refresh, or optimize pages identified as underperforming\n\n**Cost of Wrong Answer:**\n- **False Positive** (recommend fixing a page that doesn't need it): Wasted time and effort\n- **False Negative** (miss a page that needs fixing): Missed opportunity to improve traffic\n\n### Why This Question Matters\n\nContent teams manage thousands of pages but have limited time. Without data-driven guidance, they either:\n- Waste time on pages that don't need attention\n- Miss opportunities to improve high-value content\n\nA predictive model helps them focus their efforts where they matter most."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Data\n\n*Which release, which tables, date windows, what you excluded and why. Public-safe.*\n\n### Dataset\n\n**Source:** FlyRank internship warehouse (Hugging Face, gated)\n\n**Release:** `FlyRank/internship-warehouse`\n\n**Size:** 79 million rows\n\n**Tables Used:**\n- `search_console`: Search performance data (impressions, clicks, position)\n- `page_info`: Page metadata (content type, publish date)\n\n### Date Windows\n\n| Use | Date Range |\n|-----|------------|\n| **Training** | March 2026 (`month = 2026-03`) |\n| **Validation/Test** | April 2026 (`month = 2026-04`) |\n\n### What I Excluded (and Why)\n\n| Field | Why Excluded |\n|-------|--------------|\n| `query` | Contains client search data (privacy) |\n| `page_content` | Contains client content (privacy) |\n| `session_id` | Not available at prediction time |\n| `ctr` | This is the label (target) |\n| `clicks` | Label-derived (used to calculate CTR) |\n| `impressions` | Label-derived (used to calculate CTR) |\n\n### Sample Used\n\nFor this analysis, we used a sample of 10,000 pages from the dataset, accessed via streaming to manage memory constraints."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# Load and show data\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom google.colab import userdata\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, precision_score, recall_score, f1_score, roc_auc_score, confusion_matrix\nimport os\nimport warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n\nprint(\"=\"*60)\nprint(\"LOADING DATA\")\nprint(\"=\"*60)\n\ntoken = userdata.get('HF_TOKEN').strip()\n\ntry:\n    dataset = load_dataset(\n        \"FlyRank/internship-warehouse\",\n        split=\"train\",\n        streaming=True,\n        token=token\n    )\n    print(\"✅ Dataset connected!\")\n    \n    sample = []\n    for i, row in enumerate(dataset):\n        if i >= 10000:\n            break\n        sample.append(row)\n    \n    df = pd.DataFrame(sample)\n    print(f\"✅ Loaded {len(df)} rows\")\n    \nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"❌ Error: {e}\")\n    print(\"Creating simulated data for demonstration...\")\n    np.random.seed(42)\n    n = 10000\n    df = pd.DataFrame({\n        'page_id': range(1, n+1),\n        'month': np.random.choice(['2026-01', '2026-02', '2026-03', '2026-04'], n),\n        'avg_position': np.random.uniform(1, 10, n),\n        'impressions_90d': np.random.randint(0, 5000, n),\n        'content_age_days': np.random.randint(0, 365, n),\n        'content_type': np.random.choice(['article', 'video', 'product', 'news'], n),\n        'device_type': np.random.choice(['mobile', 'desktop', 'tablet'], n),\n        'ctr': np.random.uniform(0, 0.2, n),\n    })\n    df['ctr'] = df['ctr'] + (1 / (df['avg_position'] + 1)) * 0.05\n    df['ctr'] = df['ctr'].clip(0, 0.3)\n    print(f\"✅ Created {len(df)} simulated rows\")\n\nprint(\"\\nData Summary:\")\nprint(f\"Rows: {len(df)}\")\nprint(f\"Columns: {df.columns.tolist()}\")\nprint(\"\\nSample data:\")\nprint(df.head())"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Methodology\n\n*Assumptions, features, label definition, baseline, validation design, leakage checks.*\n\n### Assumptions\n\n1. **Historical data predicts future behavior:** Past CTR patterns are representative of future patterns\n2. **Search position is a strong signal:** Higher positions generally get more clicks\n3. **Freshness matters:** Newer content tends to perform better\n4. **Content type affects CTR:** Different types have different click patterns\n\n### Features Used\n\n| Feature | Type | Description | Available Before Prediction? |\n|---------|------|-------------|------------------------------|\n| `avg_position` | Numeric | Average search position | ✅ Yes - known at prediction time |\n| `impressions_90d` | Numeric | Historical impression volume | ✅ Yes - historical data |\n| `content_age_days` | Numeric | Age of content in days | ✅ Yes - known from page metadata |\n| `content_type` | Categorical | Article/Video/Product/News | ✅ Yes - known from page metadata |\n\n### Target Definition\n\n**Target:** `clicked` = 1 if CTR > median(CTR), 0 otherwise\n\n**Why this target:**\n- It is **observed** (actual user behavior, not a defined rule)\n- It is binary (yes/no click)\n- It is a valid proxy for page performance\n\n### Baseline\n\n**Week 4 Baseline Rule:**\n\n```\nScore = 0.5 × (1/(position+1)) + 0.3 × (1 - age/180) + 0.2 × (impressions/1000)\nPredict click if Score > 0.5\n```\n\nThis is a simple, hand-crafted rule that a content team could implement without ML. It serves as an honest comparison point for our model.\n\n### Model\n\n**Method:** Random Forest Classifier\n\n**Why Random Forest:**\n- Handles non-linear relationships\n- Captures feature interactions\n- Provides feature importance\n- Robust to outliers\n- Interpretable\n\n**Hyperparameters:**\n- `n_estimators`: 100\n- `max_depth`: 10\n- `min_samples_split`: 20\n- `min_samples_leaf`: 10\n- `random_state`: 42\n\n### Validation Design\n\n**Split:** Time-aware (January-March 2026 train, April 2026 test)\n\n**Why this split:**\n- Prevents future leakage\n- Simulates real-world use (predicting future from past)\n- More honest than a random split"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "print(\"=\"*60)\nprint(\"METHODOLOGY IMPLEMENTATION\")\nprint(\"=\"*60)\n\n# Prepare features\ndf['clicked'] = (df['ctr'] > df['ctr'].median()).astype(int)\n\n# One-hot encode categorical features\nif 'content_type' in df.columns:\n    df['content_type'] = df['content_type'].fillna('unknown')\n    dummies = pd.get_dummies(df['content_type'], prefix='ct')\n    df = pd.concat([df, dummies], axis=1)\n\nif 'device_type' in df.columns:\n    df['device_type'] = df['device_type'].fillna('unknown')\n    dummies = pd.get_dummies(df['device_type'], prefix='dt')\n    df = pd.concat([df, dummies], axis=1)\n\nfeature_cols = ['avg_position', 'impressions_90d', 'content_age_days']\nfeature_cols.extend([col for col in df.columns if col.startswith('ct_')])\nfeature_cols.extend([col for col in df.columns if col.startswith('dt_')])\n\nX = df[feature_cols].fillna(0)\ny = df['clicked']\n\nprint(f\"✅ Features: {len(feature_cols)}\")\nprint(f\"✅ Rows: {len(X)}\")\n\n# Time-aware split\nif 'month' in df.columns:\n    train_mask = df['month'].isin(['2026-01', '2026-02', '2026-03'])\n    test_mask = df['month'].isin(['2026-04'])\n    \n    if test_mask.sum() == 0:\n        train_mask = np.random.rand(len(df)) < 0.8\n        test_mask = ~train_mask\n        print(\"⚠️ Using random split (no April data)\")\nelse:\n    train_mask = np.random.rand(len(df)) < 0.8\n    test_mask = ~train_mask\n    print(\"⚠️ Using random split (no month column)\")\n\nX_train = X[train_mask]\nX_test = X[test_mask]\ny_train = y[train_mask]\ny_test = y[test_mask]\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n\nprint(f\"\\nSplit complete:\")\nprint(f\"  Training: {len(X_train)} rows\")\nprint(f\"  Testing: {len(X_test)} rows\")"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Results (vs baseline)\n\n*Model vs baseline on the same split. The honest table.*"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "print(\"=\"*60)\nprint(\"TRAINING MODEL\")\nprint(\"=\"*60)\n\n# Baseline Rule\ndef baseline_rule(df_subset):\n    result = pd.Series(index=df_subset.index, dtype=float)\n    \n    if 'avg_position' in df_subset.columns:\n        pos_score = 1 / (df_subset['avg_position'] + 1)\n        result = 0.5 * pos_score.clip(0, 1)\n    else:\n        result = 0.5\n    \n    if 'content_age_days' in df_subset.columns:\n        fresh_score = 1 - df_subset['content_age_days'] / 180\n        result += 0.3 * fresh_score.clip(0, 1)\n    else:\n        result += 0.3\n    \n    if 'impressions_90d' in df_subset.columns:\n        imp_score = df_subset['impressions_90d'] / 1000\n        result += 0.2 * imp_score.clip(0, 1)\n    else:\n        result += 0.2\n    \n    return (result > 0.5).astype(int)\n\n# Random Forest Model\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(\n    n_estimators=100,\n    max_depth=10,\n    min_samples_split=20,\n    min_samples_leaf=10,\n    random_state=42\n)\nrf.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\ny_pred_rf = rf.predict(X_test_scaled)\ny_proba_rf = rf.predict_proba(X_test_scaled)[:, 1]\n\n# Baseline predictions\nbaseline_pred = baseline_rule(df[X_test.index])\n\n# Metrics\nmetrics = {\n    'Accuracy': (accuracy_score, baseline_pred, y_pred_rf),\n    'Precision': (precision_score, baseline_pred, y_pred_rf),\n    'Recall': (recall_score, baseline_pred, y_pred_rf),\n    'F1 Score': (f1_score, baseline_pred, y_pred_rf),\n    'ROC-AUC': (roc_auc_score, baseline_pred, y_proba_rf)\n}\n\nprint(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*60)\nprint(\"MODEL PERFORMANCE\")\nprint(\"=\"*60)\n\nbaseline_metrics = []\nrf_metrics = []\nmetric_names = []\n\nfor name, (func, base_pred, rf_pred) in metrics.items():\n    try:\n        if name == 'ROC-AUC':\n            base_val = func(y_test, base_pred)\n        else:\n            base_val = func(y_test, base_pred, average='weighted', zero_division=0)\n        rf_val = func(y_test, rf_pred, average='weighted', zero_division=0) if name != 'ROC-AUC' else func(y_test, rf_pred)\n        \n        baseline_metrics.append(base_val)\n        rf_metrics.append(rf_val)\n        metric_names.append(name)\n        \n        print(f\"{name:12} | Baseline: {base_val:.4f} | Random Forest: {rf_val:.4f} | Improvement: {rf_val - base_val:+.4f}\")\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"{name:12} | Error: {e}\")\n\nprint(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*60)\nprint(\"FEATURE IMPORTANCE\")\nprint(\"=\"*60)\n\nimportance_df = pd.DataFrame({\n    'feature': feature_cols,\n    'importance': rf.feature_importances_\n}).sort_values('importance', ascending=False)\n\nprint(importance_df.head(10).to_string(index=False))"
  },
